odels as you can see  operates on the whole graph so it  suffers from  some drawbacks in terms of scalability  it requires the whole graph to be  processed simultaneously  so this can be very impractical for  large graphs  that contains billions of nodes and  edges such as a social network  whereas spatial do not suffer from the  scalability  issue because they directly perform  convolution  on a neighborhood basis so  together you can also incorporate some  sampling strategy  the computation can be performed in a  batch of nodes instead of  a whole graph so it can be done in  parallel  which improves efficiency for training  and the biggest limitation for spectral  is the fact that it cannot generalize to  unseen graph so any change that you make  to this graph will result in a change of  the laplacian  representation so  this means that if you change the eigen  basis and everything  you need to retrain the weights  therefore it can be impractical for  graphs that  constantly evolves or changes  whereas spatial on the other hand can be  generalized to unseen graphs quite  easily  because the aggregated weights can  induce  the embedding of unseen nodes based on  the structure  and on top of that spatial based models  are flexible  they can handle graphs with different  structures  like graphs with multiple edge or node  types  and because because these variations of  the graph input can be  incorporated into the aggregation  function  right and going back to the algorithms  of  these two approach we see that  both of them involve uh applying the  convolution layer repetitively  repeatedly sorry my english repeatedly  and the intuition for this is that every  time we apply the aggregation layer  we would propagate the information  up to one hop so the more layers you  apply  the further the information gets  propagated from its source node  therefore the richer your final  embedding would become  okay having introduced spatial  convolution  uh i am going to now introduce to you  uh two of the most popular frameworks  for  spatial convolution which is graph sage  and  graph attention networks so first we  have graph stage  graph stage is a really popular model  that is  used for inductive learning on large  graphs so  like we introduced earlier it learns  aggregator function that can induce the  embedding of a new node  given its features and neighborhood and  it trains an  aggregator function to aggregate  neighbors at different search steps  and specifically what do we mean by  aggregator function  it is a function that accepts  neighborhood as input  so specifically a set of node embeddings  from the neighborhood  and combine the embedding to create  a neighborhood aware node embedding for  the target node  as you know that the nodes neighbors  should have no  natural ordering so an aggregate data  function should be invariant  to the node ordering or permutations  and aggregate function can be both  non-parametric  such as mean pooling or max pooling  or it can be a parametric so it can  contain learnable weights like a simple  neural network  the most popular one the most popular  learnable aggregator  is a single layer neural network  followed by max pooling operator  you could also use lstm where you would  feed  the know the representation from the  neighborhood  as a sequence but you need to randomly  shuffle the order  so that your model can learn with  without overfitting to a certain order  and the propagation step which is  uh the embedding generation step for  graph sage  is really similar to what we have seen  earlier  in a spatial convolution so first you  would initialize  the node embedding in this graph to be  the initial  features then you apply multiple  convolutions  where you aggregate for each node you  aggregate  uh the features from its neighbors  then you concatenate this neighborhood  features with the features of the node  and then in the end you normalize it you  normalize  the final embedding  and yeah and you obtain  zb which is the final representation  for your target node  i've broken this down into those little  boxes  um essentially graph stage  in a nutshell is simply just those four  steps  right moving on to  training so how do we train a grass age  so you can train it in a both supervised  learning and unsupervised learning  setting  so in supervised learning you may  train it to minimize the cross-entropy  loss  and in the unsupervised learning where  you don't train with labels  you train with the assumption that  nodes which reside the same neighborhood  should have  similar embedding like this and nodes  that are independent  should be far away from each other in  the embedding space  and you could achieve this by  minimizing this loss  so j j zu  defines the loss at node u  and v is a neighbor of u  p n this should be p and u is the  negative  sampling for you so that it's a sample  of  uh nodes that are not in used  neighborhoods  so you could break this loss  into two parts the by minimizing the the  first part of the loss  you maximize the similarity so the inner  product between  uh the embedding of you and the  embedding of b  and similarly the second part  by minimizing the second part of the  loss you minimize  the similarity between z u and this  independent  node  okay so  having seen the theory of grass sage  i would like to introduce an application  for it  which is used by pinterest for post  recommendation  they adapted uh the algorithm for graph  stage  into their own framework called pinsage  where it uses an important and important  based  neighborhood sampling with random work  so that nodes with the highest visit  counts  will have highest weights in the  aggregation step  so they contribute more to the final  node representation  but alternatively you can also use  attention mechanism to determine the  importance of neighbors  so this brings us to the next variation  of graph convolution which is  graph attention network  so